description: TanStack Query server state and async data management standards
alwaysApply: true
---

# Server State Philosophy

TanStack Query is the single source of truth for server state.

The project prioritizes:
- predictable async flows
- cache consistency
- maintainable fetching logic
- smooth UX
- minimal boilerplate

Avoid:
- manual cache synchronization
- duplicated server state
- unnecessary custom fetch systems
- storing server data in Zustand

# TanStack Query Responsibilities

Use TanStack Query for:
- API fetching
- Supabase queries
- async synchronization
- mutations
- cache management
- background refetching
- optimistic updates

# Do NOT Use TanStack Query For

Avoid using TanStack Query for:
- local UI state
- modal visibility
- temporary interaction state
- form input state

Those belong to local state or Zustand.

# Query Organization

Prefer feature-based query organization.

Good:

features/
  jobs/
    hooks/
      use-jobs.ts
      use-job.ts
      use-create-job.ts

Avoid:
- giant centralized query folders
- unrelated queries mixed together

# Query Key Standards

Always use consistent query keys.

Good:

['jobs']
['jobs', jobId]
['applications', userId]
['profile', profileId]

Rules:
- keep keys predictable
- use stable identifiers
- avoid random dynamic structures

# Fetching Rules

Never fetch directly inside components when reusable.

Avoid:

useEffect(() => {
  fetch(...)
}, [])

Prefer:
- reusable hooks
- query hooks
- feature services

# Query Hook Structure

Preferred pattern:

export function useJobs() {
  return useQuery({
    queryKey: ['jobs'],
    queryFn: fetchJobs,
  })
}

# Mutation Rules

Use mutations for:
- creates
- updates
- deletes
- side-effect operations

Keep mutations:
- isolated
- explicit
- predictable

# Optimistic Updates

Use optimistic updates when UX benefits clearly.

Examples:
- chat messages
- realtime interactions
- like/favorite actions
- ride/job acceptance flows

Avoid:
- overly complex optimistic systems
- risky destructive optimistic flows

# Cache Rules

Prefer:
- query invalidation
- refetching
- normalized predictable updates

Avoid:
- manual cache hacks
- excessive direct cache mutations
- fragile synchronization logic

# Loading UX

Always handle:
- loading states
- empty states
- error states

Prefer:
- skeleton loaders
- optimistic interactions
- smooth transitions

Avoid:
- blank screens
- blocking spinners everywhere

# Error Handling

- Always handle async errors
- Use user-friendly messages
- Avoid exposing raw backend errors
- Prefer centralized reusable error handling

# Pagination Rules

For large datasets:
- use pagination
- use infinite queries when appropriate
- avoid loading huge lists at once

# Realtime Integration

Use Supabase Realtime together with TanStack Query carefully.

Preferred patterns:
- invalidate queries after realtime updates
- merge lightweight realtime updates
- keep synchronization predictable

Avoid:
- excessive manual reconciliation logic
- overly complex cache merging systems

# Query Separation

Separate:
- fetching hooks
- mutation hooks
- services
- transformations

Avoid giant all-in-one hooks.

# Service Layer Rules

Query hooks should use services.

Good:

useJobs()
  -> jobs.service.ts
  -> Supabase

Avoid:
- direct Supabase logic everywhere
- duplicated queries across components

# Retry Rules

Retries should:
- improve UX
- avoid excessive backend spam
- remain predictable

Avoid aggressive retry loops.

# Performance Rules

- Avoid unnecessary refetching
- Use staleTime appropriately
- Keep queries scoped properly
- Avoid giant reactive chains

# Maintainability Standards

Always prioritize:
- predictable async flows
- readable hooks
- isolated responsibilities
- reusable fetching patterns

Avoid:
- hidden side effects
- giant async abstractions
- complex synchronization systems

# MVP Philosophy

The async architecture should remain:
- simple
- reliable
- easy to iterate
- easy to debug

Avoid premature complexity.

# Cursor AI Expectations

When generating TanStack Query logic:
- Use reusable query hooks
- Keep async flows predictable
- Avoid duplicating server state
- Use proper query keys
- Respect feature boundaries
- Prefer maintainable fetching patterns